// Deliberate collision fixture for cratestack#777: model `Post` derives
// the free function `createPost` under `--swr`
// (`crate::naming::model_fn_names`), and `procedure create_post` derives
// the same identifier through `to_camel_case` — the snake_case spelling
// on purpose, since the collision is NOT limited to procedure names that
// are already camelCase; both run through the same `split_words`
// tokenizer. `src/swr/index.ts` `export *`s `./models/post.js` and
// `./procedures.js`, so this schema would otherwise generate a package
// that fails `tsc` with TS2308.
//
// `create`, not `list` (cratestack#784): `create` is the one operation
// whose `--swr` free function and whose generated Rust axum handler
// disagree on plurality — `createPost` against `handle_create_posts` —
// so it is the only one of the five that is a `--swr`-specific collision
// and nothing else. `procedure list_posts` (this fixture's original
// spelling) is *also* `model Post`'s `handle_list_posts`, which
// `cratestack-parser`'s `validate::procedure_handler_collisions` now
// rejects before this generator ever sees the schema — the fixture would
// no longer parse, and this file's whole point is to reach the
// generator.
//
// `@@allow("create", ...)` is load-bearing: without a `create` rule the
// model has no `create_input`, `emitted_model_fn_names` omits
// `createPost` entirely, and there would be nothing to collide with.
//
// Sibling of `swr_key_collision.cstack` (issue #344, model-vs-model file
// names) — same "fail at generation, not at the consumer's build" stance.

datasource db {
  provider = "postgresql"
  url = env("DATABASE_URL")
}

auth SessionUser {
  id Int
}

model Post {
  id Int @id
  title String

  @@allow("read", true)
  @@allow("create", auth() != null)
}

procedure create_post(): Post
  @allow(auth() != null)
